#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""repo-kit — the repo-tooling installer. ONE entry point, twelve jobs:

    repo-kit init  [--tooling PATH] [REPO]
        Scaffold a repo from the tooling templates: PLAN.md,
        project.public.yaml, CONTRIBUTING.md, adr/ format files and the
        thin caller workflows — copying ONLY what belongs in a project
        repo — then merge the tooling block into .gitignore and install
        the hooks. Existing project CONTENT files are never overwritten;
        managed files (workflows) are only written when absent, so
        re-running init is safe and reports what it skipped.

    repo-kit hooks [--tooling PATH] [REPO]
        Install/refresh .git/hooks/pre-push (the dispatcher). This is
        also the "update" path for local checks: the dispatcher reads
        the tooling checkout at push time, so new checks added to the
        toolkit apply as soon as the tooling checkout is updated.

    repo-kit uninstall [--tooling PATH] [REPO]
        Remove what `repo-kit init` installed in THIS repo: the hook
        (only when it is verifiably ours), the .gitignore block, the
        override state, the per-repo disable list, and any seeded
        workflow file still byte-identical to its template — and
        report, by name, everything it deliberately leaves (a foreign
        hook, edited files). A gating tool that cannot be cleanly
        removed will be removed badly, so removal is a first-class
        command.

    repo-kit checks [--tooling PATH] [--off NAME] [--on NAME] [REPO]
        Show or change this repo's disabled-check list (git config
        repokit.disabled-checks). The personal-mode escape hatch: a
        check that cannot run in someone's environment can be
        switched off for THIS repo instead of locking them out of
        pushing. An agent-mode (enforced) tooling checkout ignores
        the list at push time, and says so.

    repo-kit env [--tooling PATH] [--hooks-root DIR]
        Environment report: the tooling checkout's mode (personal
        or agent) and whether each external tool the checks call is
        installed, with its version — a missing tool is reported as
        the coverage gap it is. Designed to be callable from a dev
        container's entrypoint: fast and idempotent. With
        --hooks-root, also install/refresh hooks in every git repo
        under that directory.

    repo-kit setup [--tooling PATH] [--force] [answer flags]
        Guided questionnaire that builds your private
        patterns-relaxed.txt in the tooling checkout root (see "Your
        pattern list" in the tooling README). Interactive prompts with
        detected defaults (your OS username, home paths); every prompt
        has a matching flag (--email, --domain, --handle, --name,
        --age-key, --no-paths, --no-gmail, --no-tailnet) so the same
        setup is scriptable and testable. Refuses to overwrite an
        existing patterns-relaxed.txt without --force. Local checks
        add an optional patterns-strict.txt in the same place on top
        of it (a stricter, hand-kept list that never leaves your
        machine); CI only ever receives the relaxed list, as the
        GATE_PATTERNS secret.

    repo-kit provision OWNER/REPO [--variant A|B] [--dry-run]
        Apply the GitHub setup standard to a public repo through the
        `gh` CLI / `gh api`: repository settings (squash-only merges,
        auto-delete head branches, wiki/projects/discussions off unless
        --keep-*), the free security stack (secret scanning + push
        protection, Dependabot alerts + security updates, CodeQL
        default setup, private vulnerability reporting), Actions
        hardening (read-only default workflow token, approval for
        first-time contributors' fork workflows), and the
        `default-branch` ruleset from templates/rulesets/ (variant A:
        solo repo, required approvals 0; variant B: an agent or outside
        developer holds a write token — 1 approval + Code Owner review,
        with --bypass-user-id putting the owner on the bypass list in
        pull-request mode), and the GATE_PATTERNS repository secret,
        set from the tooling checkout's patterns-relaxed.txt via
        `gh secret set` (the strict list, if any, is never uploaded;
        --skip-secret opts out). When no patterns-relaxed.txt exists,
        provision offers to run `repo-kit setup` first. Finally, when
        the repo contains a dashboard caller workflow (dashboard.yml
        or self-dashboard.yml — i.e. `repo-kit init` seeded one, or
        the tooling repo itself), Pages is created or switched to be
        sourced from GitHub Actions via the Pages API (--skip-pages
        opts out); repos without a dashboard workflow skip that step
        with a note. Idempotent: an existing ruleset of the same name
        is updated in place, never duplicated. --dry-run prints every
        command and payload and executes nothing.

    repo-kit override [REPO] [--minutes N]
        Owner-only escape hatch for pattern-check false positives.
        Interactive ONLY: it refuses non-interactive shells — a
        deliberate speed bump that keeps agents and scripts from
        granting one in the normal course of things (friction plus an
        explicit human confirmation, not a security boundary: anything
        running as the owner with write access to .git could forge or
        bypass local checks entirely; the hard gate is CI). Shows the
        exact matches the check found — pattern text and matched
        lines, which the check's own output never prints — and, on
        the owner typing "override", writes a one-push allowance into
        the repo's .git directory, bound to the exact commit set
        scanned and approved (and its tip) and scoped to the
        specific patterns approved, expiring after N minutes
        (default 10, allowed 1–60). The check consumes it when the
        pattern tier applies it and prints that it did; any other
        commit set does not apply it.

    repo-kit allow-oversize [--file PATH] [--instance NAME]
                     [--minutes N] [--ceiling-mb N]
        Supervisor-side: grant the pattern service's one-shot
        oversize allowance (one scan up to N MiB, default 32,
        within N minutes, default 10) by writing the allowance
        record the service honours. Run it on the host when a
        legitimate push exceeds the service's 1 MiB scan cap.

    repo-kit enforce [on|off] [--profile full|security] [--url URL]
                     [--yes]
        Turn AGENT mode on or off for the tooling checkout (no
        argument: report the current mode). Agent mode is the mode
        for a containerized agent with its own identity: the gate
        stops believing the shell and reads its configuration only
        from supervisor-written files (hooks/enforced.marker is the
        switch; hooks/enforced.env carries the profile and the
        pattern-service coordinates). The service token is never
        taken from the command line (argv leaks into ps output and
        shell history): with --url it comes from
        $REPOKIT_ENFORCE_TOKEN, a hidden prompt, or stdin.
        Turning agent mode on for the checkout repo-kit itself
        lives in — your own, on a personal machine — needs --yes:
        your pushes from it would become agent-mode pushes. The
        intended pattern is a separate checkout for the container
        mount. Supervisors run this when building the agent's
        environment; personal installs leave it off. See "Two
        modes" in the README.

    repo-kit pattern-service [--port N] [--host ADDR]
        Run the host-side pattern service (tools/pattern_service.py)
        in the foreground: it loads the tooling checkout's pattern
        lists and answers scan requests from agent/container
        checkouts whose environment carries the three variables it
        prints at startup (REPOKIT_PATTERNS_URL, REPOKIT_PATTERNS_TOKEN,
        REPOKIT_CHECK_PROFILE=security). The lists never leave this
        machine; clients send commit text out and get tier names
        back. A token is generated per run unless
        REPOKIT_PATTERNS_TOKEN is already set. Default bind is
        loopback; for container clients, work down the bind ladder
        in the service's docstring (EXPOSURE) before 0.0.0.0.
        Ctrl-C stops it.
        Supervisors with their own lifecycle (devbox's dev-agent)
        may instead run tools/pattern_service.py directly with the
        REPOKIT_PATTERN_* environment it documents.

    repo-kit packet [packet options]
        Build a review packet (tools/review_packet.py): one
        self-contained markdown file covering a commit range — commit
        list, per-commit stats, diff, full changed-file texts — ready
        to hand to any reviewer. Packets land in the repo's
        review/packets/ folder (local-only, kept out of commits by
        review/.gitignore; reviewer feedback goes in review/feedback/).
        The tool remembers the last 10 packet endpoints in .git:
        --history lists them, --rollback N rewinds the memory N packets
        (for a packet generated but never sent), --reset clears it,
        --base REF starts one packet at a custom ref. Every packet is
        pattern-scanned before it is written, copied or recorded,
        and refused on a match (v0.4.8 fix pass, secondary review
        item 12). All packet
        options pass through: `repo-kit packet --help` shows them.

--tooling names a local tooling checkout; templates come from the
local tooling checkout (templates/ + install/ + hooks/).
"""

